          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following correctly identifies the two purine bases found in DNA?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following correctly identifies the two purine bases found in DNA?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cytosine and thymine</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cytosine and thymine are both pyrimidines — they have a single-ring structure. Remember: CUT the PY (C, U, T are pyrimidines).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adenine and guanine</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Purines (A and G) have a double-ring structure. The mnemonic 'pure as gold' links purines to A and G.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adenine and uracil</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Uracil is a pyrimidine found only in RNA, not DNA. Adenine is a purine, but its complementary base in DNA is thymine.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Guanine and cytosine</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Although G and C pair together in DNA, guanine is a purine (double ring) while cytosine is a pyrimidine (single ring). They belong to different families.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember 'pure as gold' — purines are Adenine and Guanine. Pyrimidines (C, T in DNA; C, U in RNA) have a single ring. This classification is fundamental to understanding base-pairing rules."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Purines (adenine and guanine) have a fused double-ring structure. This distinguishes them from pyrimidines (C, T, U), which have only a single ring."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
