          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>DNA replication is described as semi-conservative. What does this mean?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>DNA replication is described as semi-conservative. What does this mean?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Each new DNA molecule contains two entirely new strands</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This describes conservative replication, which was disproved by the Meselson-Stahl experiment. In that model, the original molecule would remain intact.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Only the leading strand is replicated while the lagging strand is degraded</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both strands serve as templates. The lagging strand is synthesised in short Okazaki fragments, but it is fully replicated, not discarded.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Each new DNA molecule retains one original strand and gains one newly synthesised strand</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Semi-conservative means each daughter molecule is a hybrid — one old parental strand paired with one new strand. The Meselson-Stahl experiment confirmed this model.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Replication occurs in two phases: first in the nucleus, then completed in the cytoplasm</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In eukaryotes, DNA replication occurs entirely within the nucleus during S phase. The term 'semi-conservative' refers to strand distribution, not cellular location.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "'Semi-conservative' means half is conserved in each daughter molecule — one parental strand is retained and one new strand is synthesised. This was confirmed by Meselson and Stahl's experiment using heavy nitrogen-labelled DNA."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Semi-conservative replication ensures each daughter cell receives one original template strand, maintaining fidelity across generations. This was elegantly demonstrated by Meselson and Stahl using density-labelled DNA."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
