          "label": "<p>In sickle cell anaemia, the codon GAG changes to GTG in the beta-globin gene, re</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In sickle cell anaemia, the codon GAG changes to GTG in the beta-globin gene, replacing glutamate with valine. This type of mutation is classified as a:</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Silent mutation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A silent mutation changes the codon but produces the same amino acid due to codon degeneracy. Here, the amino acid itself changes (glutamate → valine), so it is not silent.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nonsense mutation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A nonsense mutation creates a premature stop codon, truncating the protein. The GTG codon codes for valine — it is a functional amino acid, not a stop signal.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Frameshift mutation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Frameshift mutations result from insertions or deletions that shift the reading frame. In sickle cell anaemia, there is a single base substitution with no shift in reading frame.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Missense mutation</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A missense mutation substitutes one amino acid for another. The single nucleotide change (A→T) changes glutamate to valine, altering haemoglobin's properties and causing sickling.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "This is a missense mutation: one base substitution leads to one amino acid change (glutamate → valine). Unlike nonsense mutations (which create stop codons) or frameshifts (which disrupt the entire downstream reading frame), missense mutations change a single amino acid."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Sickle cell anaemia is the classic missense mutation — a single base change produces a different amino acid (Glu→Val), fundamentally altering protein function without truncating it."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
