          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In recombinant DNA technology, restriction enzymes serve which primary function?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In recombinant DNA technology, restriction enzymes serve which primary function?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Joining DNA fragments from different sources into a continuous strand</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Joining fragments is the role of DNA ligase, the 'molecular glue.' Restriction enzymes perform the opposite function — they cut DNA.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Carrying foreign DNA into the host cell for replication</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This describes vectors (plasmids, bacteriophages, cosmids). Vectors transport DNA; restriction enzymes create the fragments that vectors carry.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cutting DNA at specific palindromic recognition sequences</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Restriction enzymes are 'molecular scissors' that recognise and cut specific palindromic sequences, producing either sticky ends or blunt ends for cloning.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Synthesising complementary DNA strands during replication inside the host</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>DNA synthesis inside the host is performed by the host's own DNA polymerase. Restriction enzymes only cut — they do not build new DNA strands.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Restriction enzymes are molecular scissors — they cut DNA at specific palindromic sequences. DNA ligase joins fragments, vectors carry them into host cells, and the host's own machinery replicates the recombinant molecule."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Restriction enzymes recognise specific palindromic sequences (e.g., EcoRI cuts at GAATTC) and cut both strands, producing fragments with sticky or blunt ends that can then be joined to other DNA using ligase."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
