          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Allopurinol, the standard treatment for gout, reduces uric acid production by in</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Allopurinol, the standard treatment for gout, reduces uric acid production by inhibiting which enzyme?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adenosine deaminase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Adenosine deaminase converts adenosine to inosine in the degradation pathway. Its deficiency causes severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID), not gout.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>HGPRT is a salvage pathway enzyme that recycles purines. Its deficiency causes Lesch-Nyhan syndrome. It is not a drug target for gout.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Xanthine oxidase</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Xanthine oxidase catalyses the final two steps: hypoxanthine → xanthine → uric acid. Blocking it reduces uric acid formation and prevents crystal deposition in joints.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Guanase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Guanase converts guanine to xanthine, an earlier step in the degradation pathway. Allopurinol specifically targets xanthine oxidase, the enzyme responsible for the final conversion to uric acid.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, blocking the conversion of xanthine to uric acid. This is the standard mechanism-based treatment for gout — it reduces production rather than enhancing excretion."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Xanthine oxidase converts xanthine to uric acid — the final step in human purine catabolism. Allopurinol (and the newer febuxostat) inhibit this enzyme, reducing uric acid levels and preventing gouty arthritis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
